{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>These fire-pans of the sinners, at the cost of their lives</b> — they were held liable on account of their lives' affairs. As Reish Lakish said, for Rabbi Yohanan said: What is written — \"the godless mock at cakes\" (Job) — for the hypocrisy with which Korah flattered them regarding the matter of a drink, the prince of Gehinnom ground his teeth at them. חרק (ground). The letters of קרח (Korah)." "<b>The sinners (these)</b> — two times in the Masorah: החטאים האלה בנפשותם, את החטאים את עמלק. To teach you: just as these sinned at the cost of their lives, so Amalek sinned at the cost of his life — for he saw all the wonders done for Israel and yet engaged them, as stated in Midrash Mishle: parable of one who saw a boiling bath and jumped into it."], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The Hebrew quotes Job 24:12 בחנפי לעגי מעוג; the phrase עסקי לגימא ('matters of a drink') likely alludes to a midrashic tradition about Korah's feast. The closing notarikon reads: חרק = the letters of קרח rearranged.", 1 "The second Masorah citation (את החטאים את עמלק) alludes to 1 Samuel 15:6. The word מררש appears to be a typographical error for משלי (Proverbs) or מדרש; translated hyper-literally as 'Midrash Mishle' following the apparent intent."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:22:29.818744979Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}